Phase 1 — Local Repo Setup (already done)

mkdir -p /localdvd
mount -o loop /path/to/rhel-server-7.9-x86_64-dvd.iso /localdvd

vi /etc/yum.repos.d/local.repo


[local]
name=LocalRepository
baseurl=file:///localdvd
enabled=1
gpgcheck=0



yum clean all
yum repolist


Phase 2 — Install OS Dependencies



yum install -y libaio libaio-devel perl perl-Data-Dumper perl-DBI perl-DBD-MySQL ncurses ncurses-libs zlib



Phase 4 — Create mysql user/group


groupadd mysql
useradd -r -g mysql -s /bin/false mysql


Phase 5 — Prepare /u01 and extract tarball

mkdir -p /u01/app
mkdir -p /u01/software



Step 4: Extract into /u01/app


cd /u01/software
tar -xvzf mariadb-10.5.25-linux-systemd-x86_64.tar.gz -C /u01/app/



Step 1: Verify the extraction and check folder name


cd /u01/app
ls



Step 2: Create the symlink

cd /u01/app
ln -s mariadb-10.5.25-linux-systemd-x86_64 mysql
ls -la



Step 3: Set ownership

chown -R mysql:mysql /u01/app/mariadb-10.5.25-linux-systemd-x86_64
chown -R mysql:mysql /u01/app/mysql


Step 4: Create the data directory


mkdir -p /u01/app/mysql/data
chown -R mysql:mysql /u01/app/mysql/data


Step 5: Check the install script name

ls /u01/app/mysql/scripts/ | grep -i install


Step 6: Initialize the database (run after confirming script name)

cd /u01/app/mysql
scripts/mariadb-install-db --user=mysql --basedir=/u01/app/mysql --datadir=/u01/app/mysql/data

-- for check installation --
ls -la /u01/app
ls /u01/app/mysql/scripts/ | grep -i install


Phase 6 — Create my.cnf configuration

Step 1: Open the file

vi /etc/my.cnf


Step 2: Delete all existing content

While inside vi, make sure you're not in insert mode (press Esc first to be safe), then type:

:%d


and press Enter. This deletes every line in the file. The screen should now be blank.


Step 3: Enter insert mode

Press:

i


Step 4: Paste this content


[mysqld]
basedir=/u01/app/mysql
datadir=/u01/app/mysql/data
socket=/u01/app/mysql/mysql.sock
port=3306
user=mysql

[mysqld_safe]
log-error=/u01/app/mysql/data/mariadb.log
pid-file=/u01/app/mysql/data/mariadb.pid

Step 5: Exit insert mode and save


Press:

Esc

Then type:

:wq!

and press Enter. This saves and closes the file.


Step 6: Verify

cat /etc/my.cnf


Phase 7 — Set up the systemd service

Step 1: Verify mysqld binary location

ls /u01/app/mysql/bin/ | grep mysqld

Step 2: Create the systemd service file manually

vi /etc/systemd/system/mariadb.service


[Unit]
Description=MariaDB 10.5.25 Database Server
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=mysql
Group=mysql
ExecStart=/u01/app/mysql/bin/mysqld_safe --defaults-file=/etc/my.cnf
LimitNOFILE=10000
Restart=on-failure
PrivateTmp=false

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

Step 3: Reload systemd

systemctl daemon-reload

Step 4: Add binaries to PATH (if not done yet)


echo 'export PATH=$PATH:/u01/app/mysql/bin' >> /etc/profile.d/mariadb.sh
source /etc/profile.d/mariadb.sh




Step 5: Start the service

systemctl start mariadb
systemctl status mariadb


Step 1: Enable it to start on boot

systemctl enable mariadb

Step 2: Verify version

/u01/app/mysql/bin/mysql --version


Step 3: Secure the installation

mysql_secure_installation

Step 1: Confirm the real socket location

find / -name "mysql.sock" 2>/dev/null



Step 2: Create a symlink so the default path also works



ln -s /u01/app/mysql/mysql.sock /tmp/mysql.sock

Step 3: Now run mysql_secure_installation normally

/u01/app/mysql/bin/mysql_secure_installation --basedir=/u01/app/mysql


After that we'll do a test login:

mysql -u root



Recap: Export from Windows → Import to Linux


On Windows (HeidiSQL) — Export
Right-click the database (or select table) → Export tool → SQL export tab
Set these options:
Database(s): check Create (if you want the file to auto-create the DB too — optional)
Table(s): check Create ✅
Data: set to Insert (so actual rows get exported, not just structure)
Output: Single .sql file
Filename: browse/type a save path, e.g. C:\Users\mdbip\mariadb_export\hr_export.sql
Click Export
Confirm file exists:
cmd
   dir C:\Users\mdbip\mariadb_export\hr_export.sql
Transfer — WinSCP
Open WinSCP, connect to Linux VM
Left = Windows folder, Right = /u01/software/
Drag the .sql file across
On Linux — Fix compatibility (if needed)

Since your Windows MariaDB (12.3.2) is much newer than Linux (10.5.25), the dump may contain newer collations/syntax not supported on the older version. Fix with sed:

bash
cp hr_export.sql hr_export.sql.bak      # backup first
sed -i 's/utf8mb4_uca1400_ai_ci/utf8mb4_general_ci/g' /u01/software/hr_export.sql

(Check the error message each time — if a different unsupported feature appears, sed replace that instead.)

On Linux — Import
bash
# 1. Create the database (if the dump doesn't include CREATE DATABASE)
mysql -u root
sql
CREATE DATABASE hr;
EXIT;
bash
# 2. Import the schema + data
mysql -u root hr < /u01/software/hr_export.sql

mysql -u root < /u01/softwareemployees.sql


Verify
bash
mysql -u root
sql
USE hr;
SHOW TABLES;
SELECT * FROM employees;



-- remotely connection on window machine by DBeaver --

Step 1: Edit the config file

vi /etc/my.cnf


[mysqld]
basedir=/u01/app/mysql
datadir=/u01/app/mysql/data
socket=/u01/app/mysql/mysql.sock
port=3306
user=mysql
bind-address=0.0.0.0

[mysqld_safe]
log-error=/u01/app/mysql/data/mariadb.log
pid-file=/u01/app/mysql/data/mariadb.pid


Step 2: Verify the file saved correctly

cat /etc/my.cnf


Step 3: Restart MariaDB

systemctl restart mariadb
systemctl status mariadb


Step 4: Confirm MariaDB is listening on all interfaces


ss -tlnp | grep 3306

Step 5: Open the firewall port

firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=3306/tcp
firewall-cmd --reload
firewall-cmd --list-ports


Step 6: Create/confirm a remote-access MariaDB user

mysql -u root



CREATE USER 'remote'@'%' IDENTIFIED BY 'remote';
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON hr.* TO 'remote'@'%';
FLUSH PRIVILEGES;
EXIT;


Step 7: Get your Linux machine's IP address

ip addr show


Step 8: Connect from DBeaver (Windows)

Step 1: Open DBeaver

Step 2: Create new connection

Click the + (plug icon) in the top-left toolbar → search MariaDB → select it → Next


Step 3: Fill in these exact details

Field	        Value

Server Host	192.168.0.217
Port		3306
Database	hr
Username	remote
Password	remote


